  "id": "Compound::DB00411 - Gene::1129",
  "verbalization": "Carbachol, a compound known for its role in modulating acetylcholine signaling, is shown to bind to multiple cholinergic receptors, specifically Chrm1 and Chrm3, which are both involved in G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity. This binding leads to a downstream activation of these receptors, ultimately contributing to the activation of Chrm2. The evidence suggests that Carbachol exerts its effects through a common pathway involving G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptors, which are central to the regulation of cholinergic signaling. The presence of multiple binding sites for Carbachol on different cholinergic receptors, coupled with the shared participation in the same receptor activity, supports a mechanism where Carbachol influences Chrm2 through a common intermediary pathway. The integrated evidence highlights the importance of G-protein coupled receptors in mediating Carbachol's effects on Chrm2, emphasizing the role of these receptors in the broader context of cholinergic signaling.",
